hdad government <ei28 stopped> <ei29 providing> Ritter 's team with escorts , <ei30 making> it impossible for him to <ei31 enter> any site . Iraq <ei32 alleged> Ritter was an American spy whose team had a disproportionately high number of Americans and Britons . The official Iraqi News Agency , which gives the daily tally of inspections , did not <ei34 mention> Ritter by name , but <ei35 said> Friday that team no. 225 _ which Ritter heads _ <ei37 made> surprise <ei38 visits> to a number of sites and was <ei39 assisted> by aerial surveillance . Five other U.N. inspection teams <ei40 visited> a total of nine other sites , the agency <ei41 reported> . An Iraqi official , <ei42 speaking> on customary condition of anonymity , <ei43 said> Ritter would not <ei44 try> to <ei45 visit> any of the eight presidential sites that Baghdad had <ei46 placed> off-limits to U.N. weapons inspectors . The official <ei47 said> these sites could only be <ei48 visited> by a special team of U.N. monitors and diplomats as <ei49 laid> down by the Feb . 23 accord <ei51 signed> by U.N. Secretary - General Kofi Annan and Iraqi Deputy Prime Minister Tariq Aziz . Ritter 's <ei52 return> is <ei53 seen> as something of a <ei54 test> of that <ei55 agreement> , under which Iraq <ei56 agreed> to <ei57 give> inspectors full <ei58 access> to eight of Saddam Hussein 's presidential palaces . The United States had <ei59 moved> additional troops and a naval armada into the Gulf and <ei60 said> it would <ei61 strike> Iraq unless it gave the U.N. arms inspectors unfettered access to all potential weapons sites , including Saddam 's palaces . Iraq had <ei62 argued> the presidential sites should be off limits as symbols of sovereignty . A Pentagon spokesman <ei63 said> Thursday that the 38,000 U.S. troops in the Gulf will <ei65 remain> until Iraq <ei66 complies> with the U.N. Security Council agreement over weapons inspections . \" We are going to <ei67 maintain> our forces in the region for the foreseeable future , \" <ei68 said> spokesman Kenneth Bacon . The U.N.-Iraq accord was <ei69 worked> out by U.N. Secretary - General Kofi Annan , who <ei70 appointed> a retired Indian diplomat on Thursday as his special representative to Iraq . Prakash Shah , 58 , a former Indian ambassador to the United Nations , is part of an <ei72 effort> by Annan to <ei73 expand> contacts with the Iraqi leadership . Palace <ei74 inspections> are not <ei75 expected> to <ei76 start> until the new agreement 's procedures are in place . U.N. officials in New York on Friday <ei78 finished> <ei79 drafting> the rules , in consultation with Iraqi diplomats . The procedures are due to <ei80 go> before the Security Council next week . The Security Council has <ei82 said> it will not <ei83 lift> the sweeping <ei84 sanctions> <ei85 imposed> on Iraq after its 1990 <ei87 invasion> of Kuwait until the U.N. inspectors <ei88 certify> that Baghdad has <ei89 eliminated> its weapons of mass destruction .", "gold": [["ei28", "causes", "ei31"], ["ei55", "causes", "ei58"], ["ei87", "causes", "ei84"], ["ei31", "causedby", "ei28"], ["ei58", "causedby", "ei55"], ["ei84", "causedby", "ei87"]], "lang": "eng", "mentions": ["ei1", "ei2", "ei5", "ei6", "ei8", "ei9", "ei10", "ei11", "ei13", "ei14", "ei15", "ei16", "ei17", "ei18", "ei19", "ei21", "ei22", "ei24", "ei25", "ei26", "ei28", "ei29", "ei30", "ei31", "ei32", "ei34", "ei35", "ei37", "ei38", "ei39", "ei40", "ei41", "ei42", "ei43", "ei44", "ei45", "ei46", "ei47", "ei48", "ei49", "ei51", "ei52", "ei53", "ei54", "ei55", "ei56", "ei57", "ei58", "ei59", "ei60", "ei61", "ei62", "ei63", "ei65", "ei66", "ei67", "ei68", "ei69", "ei70", "ei72", "ei73", "ei74", "ei75", "ei76", "ei78", "ei79", "ei80", "ei82", "ei83", "ei84", "ei85", "ei87", "ei88", "ei89"], "surfaces": {"ei1": "resumed", "ei2": "work", "ei5": "blocked", "ei6": "led", "ei8": "tour", "ei9": "said", "ei10": "inspected", "ei11": "said", "ei13": "resisted", "ei14": "attempts", "ei15": "inspect", "ei16": "charged", "ei1